1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A Turing machine has seven parts: states, tape alphabet, blank symbol, input alphabet, transition function, start state, accepting states.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
One move is four things at once: read the symbol under the head, write a new one, step left or right, change state.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
A configuration is the state, the tape and the head position. The table plus the configuration fixes every later step.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
The tape is unbounded, so the machine has unlimited memory. That single change from a finite automaton is what makes it a computer.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Every run ends in one of three ways: accept, reject, or never halt. The third is not a failure of the machine; it is a legal outcome.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
A decider halts on every input. A recogniser only has to halt on the strings it accepts.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
GATE CSE 2026 Set 2 Q13 asks what "M decides L" means: accept every string in L and reject every string outside it.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Two machines can accept the same language and differ only in whether they halt. That difference is the whole subject.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
List every string in shortlex order: the empty string, then 0 and 1, then 00, 01, 10, 11, and so on. Every string gets a finite index.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
A machine description is a string, so machines can be listed the same way: M0, M1, M2, and so on. There are countably many machines.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
A language is a set of strings, so there are as many languages as subsets of the list. That is 2 to the countably many, which is uncountable.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Flip every diagonal cell of the table and you have a language that differs from every listed machine at its own string. Some languages have no machine.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
The halting problem asks: given a machine and an input, does the run end? The set of yes instances is written H.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Suppose a decider D exists: it always halts and says halt or loop. Build N, which asks D about a machine applied to itself and then does the opposite.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Run N on its own description. If D says halt, N loops. If D says loop, N halts. Both answers are wrong.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
So H is undecidable. It is still recognisable: run the machine and answer yes when it halts. Only the no answer is missing.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A reduces to B when a computable function turns every instance of A into an instance of B with the same answer.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Three implications hold and two do not. Easiness travels along the arrow; hardness travels against it.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The wrapper W ignores its input, runs M on w, and accepts when M halts. So the language of W is everything or nothing.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
That proves emptiness of a Turing machine language is undecidable. Six machine and input pairs on this page are checked against it.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Rice’s theorem: every non-trivial property of the language of a machine is undecidable. The property must be about the language.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Emptiness, universality, finiteness, regularity and "at least 21 strings" are all semantic and all non-trivial, so all are undecidable.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
But a property of the run is not a property of the language, so Rice says nothing about it. A bound of k steps is a finite table.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Four papers turn on this one line: 2020 Q26, 2021 Set 1 Q39, 2016 Set 2 Q44 and 2022 Q46.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Five classes down the side, five decision problems across: membership, emptiness, universality, equivalence, finiteness.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Regular and recursive languages answer every one of the five. Recursively enumerable languages answer none of them.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
The context-free row is the one worth memorising: membership, emptiness and finiteness decidable; universality and equivalence not.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Every undecidable cell is a theorem, not a computation. The page checks only the two cells it can: grammar emptiness and machine emptiness.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Recursive languages are closed under union, intersection, complement, concatenation and star. Every row says closed.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Recursively enumerable languages are closed under four of the five. Complement is the exception, and it is the only one.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Post’s theorem: if a language and its complement are both recognisable, the language is decidable.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The page dovetails two recognisers and decides all 31 strings of length at most 4, in at most 14 rounds.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
GATE 2020 Q26: of four languages, three are undecidable. The one about reaching a state in exactly 100 steps is decidable.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
GATE 2022 Q46: equality of two machine languages, regularity, and accepting all strings are undecidable. A 1073-step bound is not.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
GATE 2021 Set 1 Q39: "more than 2021 steps on all inputs" and "on some input" are both decidable.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
GATE 2016 Set 2 Q44: the two step-bounded languages are recursive, and "M accepts the empty string" is not.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Step one: is the question about a run or about a language? A run is simulated; a language is a candidate for Rice.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step two: is there a number in the property? A fixed bound makes it a finite table, so it is decidable.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step three: if it is semantic, is it non-trivial? Exhibit two machines with different answers, then cite Rice.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Steps four and five: fix the direction of the reduction, and read the class off the ladder. Then check the four slips.

